{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 140,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If time travel turns out to be possible, I\nhereby invite future time travelers to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4320,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "join me on set"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6100,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I’ll post the address one year from today."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8080,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay. Right now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12740,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that's a bummer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19200,
    "dDurationMs": 4360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time travel stories are cool because both\nthe past and future are somehow more interesting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23560,
    "dDurationMs": 4460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than the present and because everyone secretly wants a do-over."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28020,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But so far it appears we are doomed to live\nconsumed by regret in the eternal, boring present."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34420,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time marches on, inexorably and only forward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 38380,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or so we thought until Einstein came along."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41620,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His special and general theories of relativity\nchanged the way we think about time forever,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46690,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and believe it or not, their raw equations\npermit time travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50690,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They even tell us how to do it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 53080,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So let’s review the possibilities, and decide\nhow possible time travel really is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58300,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first approach to time travel uses only\nspecial relativity, which describes how intervals"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 63440,
    "dDurationMs": 4950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of time and space are stretched or contracted\ndepending on relative speeds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 68390,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A fast-moving spaceship appears to experience\na slower rate of time compared to someone"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 73610,
    "dDurationMs": 1990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "waiting back on Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75600,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Do a trip around the galaxy at close to the\nspeed of light and very little time might"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 79810,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pass from the perspective of the traveler."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82300,
    "dDurationMs": 4000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they’ll find a minimum of hundreds of\nthousands of years have passed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 86300,
    "dDurationMs": 1540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when they get back to Earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 87850,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However, that’s a one-way trip in time, and is really\njust traveling in the same temporal direction"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93030,
    "dDurationMs": 1730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "at different rates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94760,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So the original Planet of the Apes style time\ntravel is possible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 98700,
    "dDurationMs": 3080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it’d be nicer to be able to go back\nin time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101780,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, the math does sort of allow that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 105200,
    "dDurationMs": 6880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spaceship’s clock slows down as it speeds\nup, and it stops completely at the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112080,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And at faster speeds, time should actually\ntick backwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116700,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you could travel faster than light you\ncould navigate a path to a point in spacetime"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122369,
    "dDurationMs": 2001,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before you departed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124370,
    "dDurationMs": 4499,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We saw how to Iconstruct such a path in a\nprevious challenge episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128869,
    "dDurationMs": 4540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Of course we know that the laws of physics\nforbid faster than light travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133409,
    "dDurationMs": 1780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or do they?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135189,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order for any object with regular mass\nto even reach light speed it would need infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 140939,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy – which can never be obtained."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143579,
    "dDurationMs": 3171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But notice I said “regular mass.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146750,
    "dDurationMs": 5919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can hack the equations of special relativity\nby allowing mass to take values in the weird"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152669,
    "dDurationMs": 3091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "realm of imaginary or complex numbers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155760,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An object with imaginary mass is now restricted\nto only traveling FASTER than light, never slower."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 162660,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means it can only travel backwards in\ntime, not forwards."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167060,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call a particle with imaginary mass a tachyon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 171020,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If we could control tachyons then perhaps\nat least we could send information back in time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176000,
    "dDurationMs": 1900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But do they exist?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177900,
    "dDurationMs": 2380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Does imaginary mass exist?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180280,
    "dDurationMs": 4659,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is an example where the equations\nof a theory technically allow something to"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 184939,
    "dDurationMs": 3550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be true, but there’s still no good reason\nto believe that it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 188489,
    "dDurationMs": 4291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ve seen no evidence of tachyons, and common sense tells us we probably"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 192780,
    "dDurationMs": 1230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never will."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 194010,
    "dDurationMs": 2670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So special relativity isn’t much help."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 196680,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately we still have the general theory\nof relativity, which incorporates special relativity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 201780,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also explains the force of\ngravity as a result of curvature in the fabric"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205900,
    "dDurationMs": 3980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of spacetime due to the presence of mass and\nenergy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 209880,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But GR describes a warping of space AND time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 214280,
    "dDurationMs": 4689,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So maybe we can warp them enough to take us\nback to our own past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218969,
    "dDurationMs": 2890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The best-known approach is through something\ncalled a wormhole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221860,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A wormhole is a particularly bizarre hypothetical\nconsequence of general relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226800,
    "dDurationMs": 4780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, if space can be warped, then perhaps it can\nbe stretched in such a way as to create a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 231590,
    "dDurationMs": 5119,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "tunnel between two points – and one whose\ninternal distances could be very short, even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236709,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the mouths of the tunnel are far apart."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240089,
    "dDurationMs": 5341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This has the obvious benefit of allowing you\nto teleport between distant points in space,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245430,
    "dDurationMs": 3539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but also between distant points in time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 248969,
    "dDurationMs": 5061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is how you do it: take one stable wormhole\nlarge enough to be traversed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 254030,
    "dDurationMs": 4449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Accelerate one end to close to the speed of\nlight or drop it into a deep gravity well"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 258479,
    "dDurationMs": 4750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "– its rate of time flow will slow relative\nto the other end of the hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 263229,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now bring the two ends back together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264660,
    "dDurationMs": 7520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They will be offset in time: one portal permanently\nstuck in the past of the other by some set interval."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272180,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Travel through the “future” end\nand you’ll exit in the \"past.\""
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276750,
    "dDurationMs": 2190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So this all sounds straightforward enough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 278940,
    "dDurationMs": 2729,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But can wormholes even exist?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 281669,
    "dDurationMs": 3851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are a number of ways they might – from\nconnections between universes in the interiors"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 285520,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of black holes to miniscule wormholes appearing\nand vanishing on the tiniest scales of space and time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 291400,
    "dDurationMs": 1960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, these deserve their own episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293360,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But for now,  to build a useful time machine a wormhole has to be large enough to fit through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 298760,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it has to be stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300849,
    "dDurationMs": 6111,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The equations of GR do permit large wormholes,\nbut they are definitely not stable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306960,
    "dDurationMs": 4419,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They collapses on themselves instantly, leaving\ninescapable black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 311380,
    "dDurationMs": 5660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to keep our wormhole time machine\noperational, its throat needs to be kept open."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317040,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We need to counter gravity, and to do that\nwe need another probably-non-existent form"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322440,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of mass – negative mass – also referred\nto as exotic matter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327540,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As far as we know, mass can only take on positive,\nreal values, so a requirement of"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 332640,
    "dDurationMs": 3240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negative mass seems a non-starter."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 335880,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However there may be hope."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337620,
    "dDurationMs": 4980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Really what we need to open the wormhole\nis a negative energy density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342600,
    "dDurationMs": 5379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some have argued that we already produce this\nin the Casimir effect, in which the energy"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347980,
    "dDurationMs": 3560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the vacuum is lowered between two nearby\nconducting plates."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 351540,
    "dDurationMs": 5780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However there’s no clear path to translating\nthis to a large-scale negative energy distribution"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 357320,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that could keep a wormhole open."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 359680,
    "dDurationMs": 5979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in fact we'd need entire planets – perhaps entire stars converted to negative energy to do this."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 365659,
    "dDurationMs": 5201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some other time travel options also involve\nusing negative energy densities - for example"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 370860,
    "dDurationMs": 3709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Alcubierre warp drive, which we already\ncovered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374569,
    "dDurationMs": 4630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In short – if you have exotic matter you\ncan probably time travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379199,
    "dDurationMs": 6101,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But is negative mass-energy as much of a non-starter\nas imaginary mass?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 385300,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While the actual equations of general relativity\nthemselves don’t prohibit it, there are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389870,
    "dDurationMs": 3810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a set of secondary rules in GR that do."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393680,
    "dDurationMs": 2360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These are the so-called energy conditions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 396040,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They’re a set of requirements that do things\nlike prevent negative energies and enforce"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400840,
    "dDurationMs": 1380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy conservation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 402220,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the energy conditions don’t have a really\nfundamental basis, and they're seen to be violated"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 408100,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in some cases – like with the Casimir effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411380,
    "dDurationMs": 6740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We can’t completely rule out wormhole or\nwarp drive time machines based just on the energy conditions."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418120,
    "dDurationMs": 3720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And as it turns out, there may be other ways to build\ntime machines"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421840,
    "dDurationMs": 3600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without either negative or imaginary masses."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425449,
    "dDurationMs": 5331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One example is the Tipler cylinder, conceived\nby Frank Tipler based on a solution to the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430780,
    "dDurationMs": 2740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Einstein equations by Willem van Stockum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 433520,
    "dDurationMs": 6149,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s simple: just build an infinitely long\ncylinder of extreme density and set it rotating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 439669,
    "dDurationMs": 2831,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insanely quickly about its main axis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 442500,
    "dDurationMs": 4340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It will drag spacetime in its vicinity into\na sort of vortex."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 446840,
    "dDurationMs": 6400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This generates sub-lightspeed paths through\nspacetime that form closed loops, ending up"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453240,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back where they started in both space and\ntime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 456780,
    "dDurationMs": 4039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We call such paths closed time-like curves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460819,
    "dDurationMs": 5291,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A spaceship traveling along one of these curves\ncould return to a point in its own past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466110,
    "dDurationMs": 4651,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you don’t have the budget for an infinitely\nlong cylinder, you could try building just"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470761,
    "dDurationMs": 4458,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a very, very long cylinder… and be horribly\ndisappointed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475220,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stephen Hawking showed that unless the cylinder\nis infinitely long this doesn’t work –"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 480280,
    "dDurationMs": 2940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "unless you also modify the spacetime with negative energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483229,
    "dDurationMs": 3250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In which case you might as well just build\na wormhole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486479,
    "dDurationMs": 5852,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it turns out that it’s not so hard to\nfind solutions in general relativity with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 492340,
    "dDurationMs": 2300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "closed timelike curves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 494640,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kurt Goedel, famous for his incompleteness\ntheorem, discovered one"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498140,
    "dDurationMs": 2260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and he wasn’t even a physicist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500400,
    "dDurationMs": 5199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "His involved an entire universe, rotating\nabout a central axis and with matter and dark"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 505599,
    "dDurationMs": 4201,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "energy perfectly balancing it against collapse\nor expansion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509800,
    "dDurationMs": 3890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So to build this time machine we just need\nconstruct an entire universe – which allows"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 513690,
    "dDurationMs": 3380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "us to travel back in time only within that\nuniverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 517070,
    "dDurationMs": 1250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks Goedel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 518320,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Dragging the fabric of space in a circle\ncan give us our time-loops in very special,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 524020,
    "dDurationMs": 2100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and frankly useless cases."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526120,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Another one is the interior of a rotating\nblack hole – a so-called Kerr black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 531500,
    "dDurationMs": 7740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The maelstrom of spinning spacetime may generate\nclosed timelike curves deep down below the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539240,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So that’s fun: you can travel back to your\nown past, but never to the time before you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543980,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fell into the black hole, which is probably\nthe only thing you really want to do at that point."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548620,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unless it’s an Interstellar-style black\nhole . . . general relativity doesn’t directly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 552100,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "refute black hole time machine libraries."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555100,
    "dDurationMs": 1240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 556340,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So it seems we have lots of ways to send things\nback in time, but it all seem useless for actually making"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 562440,
    "dDurationMs": 3200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time machines for one reason or another."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565640,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the weird thing is that we don’t know\nof one consistent, fundamental law in physics"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571700,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that prohibits true time travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574130,
    "dDurationMs": 5740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And yet most physicists still think it’s\nimpossible because time travel threatens the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579870,
    "dDurationMs": 3040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "common-sense chain of cause and effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 582910,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It threatens causality."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585300,
    "dDurationMs": 5390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Break causality and you can create paradoxes–\ntime-travel to kill your grandfather and you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 590690,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "would ever be born to time travel in the first\nplace."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593800,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there are no true paradoxes – only seeming\nparadoxes that point to a gap in our understanding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600930,
    "dDurationMs": 3760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stephen Hawking put it nicely with his Chronology\nProtection Conjecture."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 604690,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It states that the laws of physics will always\nprevent time travel or allow it"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 610420,
    "dDurationMs": 2560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "only when doesn’t cause paradoxes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612980,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, the universe has to make sense,\ntime-travel or no."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617880,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One way for a closed timelike curve to exist\nwithout causing a paradox is expressed in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623530,
    "dDurationMs": 3220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Novikov Self-Consistency Principle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 626750,
    "dDurationMs": 7210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Igor Novikov suggested that closed timelike\ncurves are fine as long as they’re self-consistent."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633960,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As long as the backwards time-traveling configuration\nof matter always leads to exactly to the same"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 638920,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forward-traveling configuration."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640980,
    "dDurationMs": 3470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In other words, the loop creates itself."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644450,
    "dDurationMs": 5650,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So I don’t know – you try to kill your\ngrandfather, only to become your own grandfather?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650100,
    "dDurationMs": 4070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like Fry, let’s not think too hard about\nthat."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654170,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An alternative lies in Hugh Everett’s many-worlds\ninterpretation of quantum mechanics, in which"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659870,
    "dDurationMs": 4370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "every possible universe exists, splitting\noff in an infinite tree."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 664240,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you travel back in time and prevent\nyourself being born – no problem - your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 668530,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "photo doesn’t slowly fade away because you\nwere still born in that other timeline."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 673980,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or time-travel could be genuinely impossible."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 677100,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kip Thorn suggests there should probably be\none fundamental law of physics that prohibits it -"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 682940,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "for example, the quantum vacuum may\nbe unstable in the infinitely iterating loops"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 688520,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of a closed timelike curves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 691360,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand, Kip was the consultant\nin Interstellar, so who knows what to believe?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696580,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In actual fact we can’t know until we have\na full theory of quantum gravity –"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702080,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "until then we’re working with the approximate\ntheories general relativity and quantum theory."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 707180,
    "dDurationMs": 6020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Approximate theories can make bad predictions\n– like the possibility of time travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 713200,
    "dDurationMs": 6460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "One final argument that time travel is impossible\nis that we don’t see time travelers."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719660,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Stephen Hawking put this to rigorous test\nwhen he organized a cocktail party for time"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 724580,
    "dDurationMs": 4300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "travelers, only advertising the event after\nit ended."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 728880,
    "dDurationMs": 2280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Tellingly, no one showed up."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731160,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Though I don’t know – maybe there was a slightly\nbetter party somewhere else in, like,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735240,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "all of history."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736800,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For now we seem doomed to time travel only\nforwards, and very slowly at that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 741760,
    "dDurationMs": 7800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We remain firmly in the grip of that one dimension\nthat we can never halt nor reverse it's pace: time."
    } ]
  } ]
}
